//!
//! # Scheduler
//!
//! Kernel threads on every processor, switched cooperatively and by the timer.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! A thread is a slot in the fixed table of `thread.ign`: an id, a state, the
//! stack pointer it was switched out with, and a kernel stack of its own. Slot
//! 0 is the boot thread, slot 1 is the idle thread, which runs only when
//! nothing else is ready, and every other slot is free until `spawn` fills it.
//! This file decides which slot runs.
//!
//! Ready threads wait in a FIFO run queue and run in the order they entered
//! it. Once `enablePreemption` is called, the timer interrupt also takes the
//! processor from a thread that has used `TIME_SLICE_TICKS` ticks, so a
//! thread that never yields cannot starve the others. A thread leaves the
//! processor by calling `yield` (back to the end of the queue), `sleep`
//! (blocked until a tick count) or by returning from its function, which ends
//! in `exitCurrent`. The stack of a dead thread cannot be freed while it is
//! still running on it, so the dead thread is recorded and the next thread to
//! run frees it right after the switch.
//!
//! One spinlock, the run queue lock (rank `Lib::Rank::RUN_QUEUE`), guards
//! the queue, the thread table and every thread's state. It is taken with
//! interrupts already off, and each thread keeps the interrupt state it saved
//! on its own stack, so a thread that switches out with interrupts off and is
//! resumed later restores its own state, not the state of the thread that
//! ran before.
//!
//! The lock is held across a context switch and released by the thread
//! switched in: right after `Arch::Context::switchTo` returns in it, or in
//! `threadBegin` for a new thread. Until then the outgoing thread's
//! registers are not saved, and no other processor may pick it up. A tick
//! that finds any spinlock held on its CPU touches no scheduler state and
//! puts the switch off to the first tick after the release.
//!
//! ```text
//!   thread A                        thread B
//!   lockRunQueue()
//!   state = READY, enqueue
//!   switchToNext() ── switchTo ──►  (returns in its own switchToNext,
//!                                    or starts in threadBegin)
//!                                   unlockRunQueue()
//! ```
//!
//! A thread may own an address space, the physical address of a PML4 made by
//! `Mm::AddressSpace::create`. Every switch loads the incoming thread's PML4, or
//! the kernel's when it owns none, and points TSS.RSP0 and the `SYSCALL`
//! stack (`gs:[8]` of the per-CPU block) at the incoming thread's kernel
//! stack, so an interrupt or a system call from ring 3 lands on it.
//!
//! The threads of one user process share its space (`spawnMember`). The
//! reap of a dead thread destroys its space only when no other thread in
//! the table still holds it, so the space goes with the last of them, once:
//!
//! ```text
//!   thread A (root R) dies ─► reapZombie: B still holds R ─► R stays
//!   thread B (root R) dies ─► reapZombie: nobody holds R  ─► destroy(R)
//! ```
//!
//! `awaitSharers` lets the last thread of a process wait until its siblings
//! are reaped, so the reap of that thread is the one that destroys.
//!
//! Every switch also saves the outgoing thread's x87 and SSE registers and
//! loads the incoming one's (`Arch::Fpu`), so a user program's vector and
//! floating point registers are its own.
//!
//! ## Preemption
//!
//! ```text
//!   LAPIC timer ──► vector 0x30 ──► Trap::dispatch
//!                                     ├─ Time::Timer::handleTick       ticks += 1
//!                                     ├─ Apic::Lapic::endOfInterrupt
//!                                     └─ Sched::Scheduler::onTick       (interrupts still off)
//!                                           ├─ count the tick against the thread
//!                                           ├─ a spinlock held? stop here
//!                                           ├─ take the run queue lock, wake sleepers
//!                                           └─ slice over and others ready?
//!                                                  makeReady(current); switchToNext()
//!
//!   switchToNext() leaves the interrupted thread's whole call chain on its own
//!   kernel stack (interrupt frame, dispatch, onTick, switchToNext). The
//!   thread resumes there later and finishes its own interrupt return.
//! ```
//!
//! See concept doc 13.

import Apic from "@apic";
import Arch from "@arch";
import Lib from "@lib";
import Mm from "@mm";
import Time from "@time";
import _ from "./thread";

/// The scheduler state. The array sizes must equal `Sched::Scheduler::MAX_THREADS`,
/// and have to be integer literals.
record SchedulerState {
  /// The run queue: a ring buffer of slot numbers, shared by every
  /// processor.
  static mut queue: u64[80];

  /// Index in `queue` of the next thread to run.
  static mut queueHead: u64;

  /// Threads in the run queue.
  static mut queueLength: u64;

  /// Context switches since boot.
  static mut switches: u64;

  /// Times the timer took the processor from a thread that used its slice.
  static mut preemptions: u64;

  /// Ticks left in the time slice of the thread running on processor n.
  /// The running slot and the zombie of each processor live in its per-CPU
  /// block (`Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot`, `zombie`). Sizes equal
  /// `Arch::CpuLocal::MAX_CPUS`.
  static mut sliceLeft: u64[8];

  /// True once `initialize` has built the table, so `onTick` may run.
  static mut started: boolean;

  /// True once `enablePreemption` has been called.
  static mut preemptive: boolean;

  /// Ticks of processor n that would have switched threads but found a
  /// spinlock held.
  static mut deferred: u64[8];

  /// Bit n set while processor n runs its idle thread. Changed under the run
  /// queue lock.
  static mut idleMask: u64;

  /// The local APIC id of processor n, for the reschedule IPI.
  static mut lapicIds: u64[8];

  /// Reschedule IPIs sent.
  static mut ipisSent: u64;

  /// Reschedule IPIs that found work and switched an idle processor to it.
  static mut ipiSwitches: u64;

  /// The run queue lock, rank `Lib::Rank::RUN_QUEUE`. It guards the queue,
  /// the thread table and every thread's state, and is held across a context
  /// switch: the thread switched in releases it.
  static mut lock: Arch::SpinLock;
}

/// The scheduler API.
namespace Sched::Scheduler {
  /// Timer ticks a thread may run before the timer interrupt hands the
  /// processor to the next ready thread: 10 ticks at 100 Hz is 100 ms.
  const TIME_SLICE_TICKS: u64 = 10;

  /// Builds the table with the boot thread running and the idle thread
  /// ready. The kernel page tables and the PMM must be up.
  function initialize(): void {
    Arch::Spin::initialize(runQueueLock(), Lib::Rank::RUN_QUEUE);
    Sched::Thread::initializeBootThread();

    Arch::CpuLocal::setCurrentSlot(Sched::Thread::BOOT_SLOT);

    if (!Sched::Thread::createThread(Sched::Thread::IDLE_SLOT, Arch::ContextAsm::ignis_function_address(idleLoop), 0, 0)) {
      @panic("scheduler: cannot create the idle thread");
    }

    SchedulerState::started = true;
  }

  /// Lets the timer interrupt switch threads. Until it is called, threads
  /// change only when one of them yields, sleeps or exits.
  function enablePreemption(): void {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    *sliceLeft() = TIME_SLICE_TICKS;
    SchedulerState::preemptive = true;

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// Called from the timer interrupt after the end-of-interrupt, with
  /// interrupts still disabled by the interrupt gate. Counts the tick
  /// against the running thread, wakes sleepers, and switches threads when
  /// the time slice is over or the idle thread is running while another
  /// thread is ready, unless this CPU holds a spinlock: then the switch
  /// waits for the first tick after the release. The interrupt then returns
  /// in whichever thread was switched in, which finishes its own interrupt
  /// return later.
  function onTick(): void {
    if (!SchedulerState::started || !SchedulerState::preemptive) {
      return;
    }

    let current: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());

    (*current).runTicks += 1;

    // A spinlock is held on this CPU: the interrupted code may be in the
    // middle of the run queue itself, so the tick touches nothing shared and
    // switches nothing. The slice stays used up, so the switch happens on
    // the first tick after the release; sleepers wake on that tick too.
    if (Arch::Spin::holdsAny()) {
      if (*sliceLeft() > 0) {
        *sliceLeft() -= 1;
      }

      if (*sliceLeft() == 0 && !Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())) {
        *deferredOf(Arch::CpuLocal::index()) += 1;
      }

      return;
    }

    lockRunQueue();
    wakeSleepers();

    if (Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())) {
      if (SchedulerState::queueLength > 0) {
        makeReady(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());
        switchToNext();
      }

      unlockRunQueue();
      return;
    }

    if (*sliceLeft() > 0) {
      *sliceLeft() -= 1;
    }

    if (*sliceLeft() > 0) {
      unlockRunQueue();
      return;
    }

    if (SchedulerState::queueLength == 0) {
      *sliceLeft() = TIME_SLICE_TICKS;
      unlockRunQueue();
      return;
    }

    SchedulerState::preemptions += 1;
    makeReady(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());
    switchToNext();
    unlockRunQueue();
  }

  /// Creates a thread that runs `entry(argument)` and puts it at the end of
  /// the run queue. `entry` comes from `Arch::ContextAsm::ignis_function_address`.
  /// Returns the thread id, or `NO_THREAD` when every slot is taken or the
  /// stack cannot be mapped.
  function spawn(entry: u64, argument: u64): u64 {
    return spawnIn(entry, argument, 0);
  }

  /// `spawn` for a thread that runs in the address space `root`, which the
  /// thread then owns: it is destroyed when the thread ends, and also when
  /// the thread cannot be created. 0 means the kernel's own address space.
  function spawnIn(entry: u64, argument: u64, root: u64): u64 {
    let id: u64 = spawnMember(entry, argument, root, 0);

    if (id == Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD && root != 0) {
      Mm::AddressSpace::destroy(root);
    }

    return id;
  }

  /// `spawn` for a thread of the process slot `processPlusOne` - 1 (0 for
  /// none) that runs in `root`, which it shares with every other thread
  /// holding it: the reap of the last of them destroys it. Nothing is
  /// destroyed when the thread cannot be created.
  function spawnMember(entry: u64, argument: u64, root: u64, processPlusOne: u64): u64 {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    let slot: u64 = Sched::Thread::findFreeSlot();

    if (slot == Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD || !Sched::Thread::createThread(slot, entry, argument, root)) {
      Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());
      return Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD;
    }

    (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot)).process = processPlusOne;
    enqueue(slot);
    kickIdleProcessor();

    let id: u64 = (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot)).id;

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());

    return id;
  }

  /// Gives the processor to the next ready thread and goes to the end of the
  /// queue. Returns at once when no other thread is ready.
  function yield(): void {
    noSpinlockHeld();

    let saved: u64 = Arch::Cpu::saveAndDisableInterrupts();

    lockRunQueue();
    wakeSleepers();

    if (SchedulerState::queueLength > 0) {
      makeReady(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());
      switchToNext();
    }

    unlockRunQueue();
    Arch::Cpu::restoreInterrupts(saved);
  }

  /// Blocks the calling thread for at least `ticks` timer ticks. Sleeping
  /// zero ticks is a yield. The idle thread must not sleep.
  function sleep(ticks: u64): void {
    noSpinlockHeld();

    if (ticks == 0) {
      yield();
      return;
    }

    if (Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())) {
      @panic("scheduler: the idle thread cannot sleep");
    }

    let saved: u64 = Arch::Cpu::saveAndDisableInterrupts();
    let current: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());

    lockRunQueue();

    if (!takeInterrupt(current)) {
      (*current).wakeTick = Time::Timer::ticks() + ticks;
      (*current).state = Sched::Thread::BLOCKED;
      switchToNext();
    }

    unlockRunQueue();

    Arch::Cpu::restoreInterrupts(saved);
  }

  /// Blocks the calling thread until the thread `id` has ended and its
  /// stack and address space are freed. Returns at once when no thread with
  /// that id is in the table. The caller is not switched in again before
  /// then: the thread that frees the dead one (`reapZombie`) makes the
  /// waiters ready. A thread cannot wait for itself, and the idle thread
  /// must not wait.
  function join(id: u64): void {
    noSpinlockHeld();

    if (id == currentId()) {
      @panic("scheduler: a thread cannot wait for itself");
    }

    if (Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())) {
      @panic("scheduler: the idle thread cannot wait");
    }

    let saved: u64 = Arch::Cpu::saveAndDisableInterrupts();

    lockRunQueue();

    let current: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());

    if (Sched::Thread::isAlive(id) && !takeInterrupt(current)) {
      (*current).joinTarget = id;
      (*current).state = Sched::Thread::WAITING;
      switchToNext();
    }

    unlockRunQueue();
    Arch::Cpu::restoreInterrupts(saved);
  }

  /// Ends the calling thread. The trampoline calls it when a thread function
  /// returns. It never returns: the stack is freed by the next thread that
  /// runs.
  @externName("ignis_thread_exit")
  function exitCurrent(): never {
    noSpinlockHeld();

    Arch::Cpu::disableInterrupts();

    if (Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot() == Sched::Thread::BOOT_SLOT || Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())) {
      @panic("scheduler: the boot or idle thread cannot exit");
    }

    lockRunQueue();
    (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())).state = Sched::Thread::DEAD;
    Arch::CpuLocal::setZombie(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot() + 1);
    switchToNext();

    @panic("scheduler: a dead thread was resumed");
  }

  /// Called by the trampoline in a thread's first moments, still with
  /// interrupts disabled and the run queue lock that the switch handed over
  /// held: frees the thread that ran before if it died, then releases the
  /// lock.
  @externName("ignis_thread_begin")
  function threadBegin(): void {
    reapZombie();
    unlockRunQueue();
  }

  /// True once `initialize` has built the thread table: from then on other
  /// threads may run.
  function isStarted(): boolean {
    return SchedulerState::started;
  }

  /// The id of the running thread.
  function currentId(): u64 {
    return (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())).id;
  }

  /// How many times the processor changed threads.
  function switchCount(): u64 {
    return SchedulerState::switches;
  }

  /// How many times the timer interrupt took the processor from a thread
  /// that had used its time slice.
  function preemptionCount(): u64 {
    return SchedulerState::preemptions;
  }

  /// The idle thread: sleeps until an interrupt and then lets anything that
  /// became ready run.
  function idleLoop(argument: u64): void {
    while (true) {
      yield();

      // A reschedule IPI clears this processor's idle bit; one that came
      // while a spinlock was held did nothing, so the bit is set again
      // before every sleep, or later wake-ups would skip this processor.
      Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());
      markIdle(true);
      Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());

      Arch::Cpu::waitForInterrupt();
    }
  }

  /// Frees the stack and the slot of the thread that died just before the
  /// current one was switched in, and its address space when no other
  /// thread holds it. The run queue lock is held: the stack and the address
  /// space are freed under it (page tables, heap and frames rank above it),
  /// and nothing in that path may sleep.
  function reapZombie(): void {
    let marker: u64 = Arch::CpuLocal::zombie();

    if (marker == 0) {
      return;
    }

    Arch::CpuLocal::setZombie(0);

    let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(marker - 1);

    // The stack stays mapped for the next thread in the slot (see
    // `thread.ign`, "Thread Stacks").
    if ((*thread).root != 0) {
      if (sharerOf(marker - 1, (*thread).root) == Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD) {
        Mm::AddressSpace::destroy((*thread).root);
      }

      (*thread).root = 0;
    }

    // The scratch area stays with the slot and serves the next thread
    // created in it.
    (*thread).state = Sched::Thread::UNUSED;

    wakeJoiners((*thread).id);
  }

  /// Parks the running thread until another thread calls `wake` on it, and
  /// returns then. A `wake` that came after the caller checked its condition
  /// but before the park left a token, and the park returns at once, so no
  /// wake-up is lost. The caller checks its condition again in a loop:
  /// a stale token can end a park early.
  function park(): void {
    noSpinlockHeld();

    if (Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())) {
      @panic("scheduler: the idle thread cannot park");
    }

    let saved: u64 = Arch::Cpu::saveAndDisableInterrupts();

    let current: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());

    lockRunQueue();

    if ((*current).wakePending != 0) {
      (*current).wakePending = 0;
    } else if (!takeInterrupt(current)) {
      (*current).state = Sched::Thread::PARKED;
      switchToNext();
    }

    unlockRunQueue();

    Arch::Cpu::restoreInterrupts(saved);
  }

  /// Makes the thread `id` ready when it is parked. Otherwise leaves a token
  /// that its next `park` consumes and returns on.
  function wake(id: u64): void {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS) {
      let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot);

      if ((*thread).id == id && (*thread).state != Sched::Thread::UNUSED) {
        wakeSlot(slot);
      }

      slot += 1;
    }

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// `wake` for every thread of the process slot `processPlusOne` - 1.
  function wakeProcess(processPlusOne: u64): void {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS) {
      if (isMemberSlot(slot, processPlusOne)) {
        wakeSlot(slot);
      }

      slot += 1;
    }

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// Makes the parked thread in `slot` ready, or leaves it a token. The run
  /// queue lock is held.
  function wakeSlot(slot: u64): void {
    let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot);

    if ((*thread).state == Sched::Thread::PARKED) {
      makeReady(slot);
    } else {
      (*thread).wakePending = 1;
    }
  }

  /// Makes the thread `id` ready if it waits in `sleep`, `join` or `park`,
  /// so it can notice that it was killed. Each of those returns early; its
  /// caller checks why. A thread that is ready or running gets a token
  /// instead, and its next `sleep`, `join` or `park` returns at once, so a
  /// kill that lands between a check and a wait is not lost. A thread in a
  /// wait queue is left alone.
  function interrupt(id: u64): void {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS) {
      if ((*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot)).id == id) {
        interruptSlot(slot);
      }

      slot += 1;
    }

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// `interrupt` for every thread of the process slot `processPlusOne` - 1
  /// except the thread `except`.
  function interruptProcess(processPlusOne: u64, except: u64): void {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS) {
      if (isMemberSlot(slot, processPlusOne) && (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot)).id != except) {
        interruptSlot(slot);
      }

      slot += 1;
    }

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// Makes the thread in `slot` ready when it waits in `sleep`, `join` or
  /// `park`, or gives it the interrupt token when it is ready or running.
  /// The run queue lock is held.
  function interruptSlot(slot: u64): void {
    let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot);
    let state: u64 = (*thread).state;

    if (state == Sched::Thread::BLOCKED || state == Sched::Thread::WAITING || state == Sched::Thread::PARKED) {
      makeReady(slot);
    } else if (state == Sched::Thread::READY || state == Sched::Thread::RUNNING) {
      (*thread).interrupted = 1;
    }
  }

  /// The id of a thread of the process slot `processPlusOne` - 1 still in
  /// the table, or `NO_THREAD`.
  function threadOfProcess(processPlusOne: u64): u64 {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    let mut found: u64 = Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD;
    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS && found == Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD) {
      if (isMemberSlot(slot, processPlusOne)) {
        found = (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot)).id;
      }

      slot += 1;
    }

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());

    return found;
  }

  /// True when `slot` holds a thread, not dead, of the process slot
  /// `processPlusOne` - 1 (above 0). The run queue lock is held.
  function isMemberSlot(slot: u64, processPlusOne: u64): boolean {
    let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot);

    return processPlusOne != 0 && (*thread).process == processPlusOne && (*thread).state != Sched::Thread::UNUSED && (*thread).state != Sched::Thread::DEAD;
  }

  /// The slot of a thread other than the one in `slot` that holds the
  /// address space `root`, dead and not yet reaped included, or
  /// `NO_THREAD`. The run queue lock is held.
  function sharerOf(slot: u64, root: u64): u64 {
    let mut other: u64 = 0;

    while (other < Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS) {
      let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(other);

      if (other != slot && (*thread).state != Sched::Thread::UNUSED && (*thread).root == root) {
        return other;
      }

      other += 1;
    }

    return Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD;
  }

  /// Blocks the calling thread until no other thread holds its address
  /// space, so that its own reap destroys it. The last thread of a process
  /// calls it before it ends; its siblings already left and only their
  /// reaps are pending. A kill does not end the wait.
  function awaitSharers(): void {
    noSpinlockHeld();

    let saved: u64 = Arch::Cpu::saveAndDisableInterrupts();

    lockRunQueue();

    let me: u64 = Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot();
    let current: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(me);

    while ((*current).root != 0 && sharerOf(me, (*current).root) != Sched::Thread::NO_THREAD) {
      (*current).joinTarget = (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(sharerOf(me, (*current).root))).id;
      (*current).state = Sched::Thread::WAITING;
      switchToNext();
    }

    unlockRunQueue();
    Arch::Cpu::restoreInterrupts(saved);
  }

  /// Clears `thread`'s interrupt token and returns true when it was set. The
  /// run queue lock is held.
  function takeInterrupt(thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control): boolean {
    if ((*thread).interrupted == 0) {
      return false;
    }

    (*thread).interrupted = 0;

    return true;
  }

  /// Records which process slot the running thread runs: the slot plus 1, or
  /// 0 for none. `spawnMember` sets it; the thread clears it when it leaves
  /// its process, under the process table lock.
  function setProcess(slotPlusOne: u64): void {
    (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())).process = slotPlusOne;
  }

  /// What `setProcess` recorded for the running thread.
  function currentProcess(): u64 {
    return (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())).process;
  }

  /// Panics when this CPU holds a spinlock: a thread must not give up the
  /// processor with one held, or others would spin until it runs again.
  function noSpinlockHeld(): void {
    if (Arch::Spin::holdsAny()) {
      @panic("scheduler: a thread tried to sleep or yield while holding a spinlock");
    }
  }

  /// The running thread's scratch area, `Sched::Thread::SCRATCH_BYTES` bytes
  /// no other thread touches.
  function scratch(): *mut u8 {
    return Sched::Thread::scratchOf(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());
  }

  /// The slot of the running thread.
  function currentSlot(): u64 {
    return Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot();
  }

  /// Ticks whose switch was put off because a spinlock was held.
  function deferredCount(): u64 {
    let mut total: u64 = 0;
    let mut cpu: u64 = 0;

    while (cpu < Arch::CpuLocal::MAX_CPUS) {
      total += *deferredOf(cpu);
      cpu += 1;
    }

    return total;
  }

  /// The slice counter of the running processor. Interrupts are off or the
  /// run queue lock is held, so the thread cannot move meanwhile.
  function sliceLeft(): *mut u64 {
    return &mut SchedulerState::sliceLeft as *mut u64 + Arch::CpuLocal::index();
  }

  /// The deferred-switch counter of processor `cpu`.
  function deferredOf(cpu: u64): *mut u64 {
    return &mut SchedulerState::deferred as *mut u64 + cpu;
  }

  /// Creates the idle thread of processor `cpu`, before the processor joins
  /// (`startProcessor`). Runs on the boot processor.
  function addProcessor(cpu: u64): void {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    let created: boolean = Sched::Thread::createThread(Sched::Thread::idleSlot(cpu), Arch::ContextAsm::ignis_function_address(idleLoop), 0, 0);

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());

    if (!created) {
      @panic("scheduler: cannot create a processor's idle thread");
    }
  }

  /// Makes the running application processor join the scheduler: it becomes
  /// its idle thread, the way a switch would enter it, holding the run
  /// queue lock that `threadBegin` releases. Interrupts must be off. Never
  /// returns; the stack it runs on is abandoned.
  function startProcessor(): void {
    let cpu: u64 = Arch::CpuLocal::index();
    let idle: u64 = Sched::Thread::idleSlot(cpu);
    let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(idle);
    let mut abandoned: u64 = 0;

    lockRunQueue();
    (*thread).state = Sched::Thread::RUNNING;
    (*thread).cpu = cpu;
    Arch::CpuLocal::setCurrentSlot(idle);
    markIdle(true);
    *sliceLeft() = TIME_SLICE_TICKS;
    Arch::Fpu::restore(Sched::Thread::fpuArea(idle) as *u8);
    Arch::Context::switchTo(&mut abandoned as *mut u64, (*thread).savedRsp);

    @panic("scheduler: a processor's boot stack was resumed");
  }

  /// True while a thread with this id is in the table, dead or not yet
  /// freed included. Takes the run queue lock.
  function isAlive(id: u64): boolean {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());

    let alive: boolean = Sched::Thread::isAlive(id);

    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());

    return alive;
  }

  /// Takes the run queue lock with `lockIrq`, for code outside the scheduler
  /// that reads the thread table (the `threads` command). Nothing in between
  /// may sleep.
  function lockTable(): void {
    Arch::Spin::lockIrq(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// Releases what `lockTable` took.
  function unlockTable(): void {
    Arch::Spin::unlockIrq(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// Takes the run queue lock. Interrupts must already be off: the caller
  /// keeps its own interrupt state on its own stack, because the lock may
  /// be released by another thread after a switch.
  function lockRunQueue(): void {
    Arch::Spin::lock(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// Releases the run queue lock, in the thread that took it or in the
  /// thread a switch handed it to.
  function unlockRunQueue(): void {
    Arch::Spin::unlock(runQueueLock());
  }

  /// `SchedulerState::lock`.
  function runQueueLock(): *mut Arch::SpinLock {
    return &mut SchedulerState::lock as *mut Arch::SpinLock;
  }

  /// True when this CPU holds the run queue lock and no other spinlock, the
  /// state every switch needs.
  function holdsOnlyRunQueue(): boolean {
    return SchedulerState::lock.locked != 0 && Arch::CpuLocal::preemptCount() == 1 && Arch::CpuLocal::spinTop() == Lib::Rank::RUN_QUEUE;
  }

  /// Makes every thread that waits for the thread `id` ready. The run queue
  /// lock is held.
  function wakeJoiners(id: u64): void {
    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS) {
      let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot);

      if ((*thread).state == Sched::Thread::WAITING && (*thread).joinTarget == id) {
        makeReady(slot);
      }

      slot += 1;
    }
  }

  /// Loads what the thread in `slot` runs on: its page tables, and, when it
  /// owns an address space, its kernel stack as the one ring 3 enters on.
  function activateSpaceOf(slot: u64): void {
    let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot);
    let mut root: u64 = (*thread).root;

    if (root == 0) {
      root = Mm::Vmm::rootTable();
    } else {
      Arch::UserMode::setKernelStack((*thread).stackBase + Sched::Thread::STACK_SPAN);
    }

    if (Arch::Cpu::readCr3() != root) {
      Arch::Cpu::writeCr3(root);
    }
  }

  /// Picks the next thread, which is the head of the run queue or the idle
  /// thread, and switches to it. The caller has already put the current
  /// thread in the state it leaves in: ready and queued, blocked, or dead.
  /// Interrupts must be disabled and the caller holds the run queue lock and
  /// no other spinlock. Returns when the calling thread is switched back in,
  /// holding the run queue lock again: the thread that switched to it took
  /// it, and the caller releases it.
  function switchToNext(): void {
    if (!holdsOnlyRunQueue()) {
      @panic("scheduler: a switch needs the run queue lock and no other spinlock");
    }

    wakeSleepers();

    let outgoing: u64 = Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot();
    let mut next: u64 = Sched::Thread::idleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::index());

    if (SchedulerState::queueLength > 0) {
      next = dequeue();
    }

    (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(next)).state = Sched::Thread::RUNNING;
    (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(next)).cpu = Arch::CpuLocal::index();
    markIdle(Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(next));
    *sliceLeft() = TIME_SLICE_TICKS;

    if (next == outgoing) {
      return;
    }

    Arch::CpuLocal::setCurrentSlot(next);
    SchedulerState::switches += 1;

    activateSpaceOf(next);

    // The kernel never uses the x87 and SSE registers, so the incoming
    // thread's state can be loaded before the stack switch.
    Arch::Fpu::save(Sched::Thread::fpuArea(outgoing));
    Arch::Fpu::restore(Sched::Thread::fpuArea(next) as *u8);

    Arch::Context::switchTo(&mut (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(outgoing)).savedRsp as *mut u64, (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(next)).savedRsp);

    reapZombie();
  }

  /// Moves every sleeper whose time has come to the run queue. The run queue
  /// lock is held.
  function wakeSleepers(): void {
    let now: u64 = Time::Timer::ticks();
    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS) {
      let thread: *mut Sched::Thread::Control = Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot);

      if ((*thread).state == Sched::Thread::BLOCKED && (*thread).wakeTick <= now) {
        makeReady(slot);
      }

      slot += 1;
    }
  }

  /// Marks `slot` ready and queues it. The idle thread is never queued: it
  /// runs when the queue is empty. The run queue lock is held.
  function makeReady(slot: u64): void {
    (*Sched::Thread::slotPointer(slot)).state = Sched::Thread::READY;

    if (!Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(slot)) {
      enqueue(slot);
      kickIdleProcessor();
    }
  }

  /// Sends a reschedule IPI to one other processor that is idle, so the
  /// thread just queued runs now instead of at that processor's next tick.
  /// The processor's idle bit is cleared, so a burst of wake-ups sends one
  /// IPI per idle processor; it is set again when the processor next goes
  /// idle. The run queue lock is held.
  function kickIdleProcessor(): void {
    let others: u64 = SchedulerState::idleMask & ~(1 << Arch::CpuLocal::index());

    if (others == 0) {
      return;
    }

    let mut cpu: u64 = 0;

    while ((others & (1 << cpu)) == 0) {
      cpu += 1;
    }

    SchedulerState::idleMask = SchedulerState::idleMask & ~(1 << cpu);
    SchedulerState::ipisSent += 1;
    Apic::Lapic::sendIpi(*(&mut SchedulerState::lapicIds as *mut u64 + cpu), Apic::Lapic::RESCHEDULE_VECTOR);
  }

  /// Records whether the running processor now runs its idle thread. The
  /// run queue lock is held.
  function markIdle(idle: boolean): void {
    let bit: u64 = 1 << Arch::CpuLocal::index();

    if (idle) {
      SchedulerState::idleMask = SchedulerState::idleMask | bit;
    } else {
      SchedulerState::idleMask = SchedulerState::idleMask & ~bit;
    }
  }

  /// The reschedule IPI handler, with interrupts off: an idle processor
  /// switches to the head of the run queue. Does nothing while a spinlock
  /// is held on this processor, or when it is busy or nothing waits.
  function onReschedule(): void {
    if (!SchedulerState::started || Arch::Spin::holdsAny()) {
      return;
    }

    lockRunQueue();

    if (Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot()) && SchedulerState::queueLength > 0) {
      SchedulerState::ipiSwitches += 1;
      makeReady(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot());
      switchToNext();
    } else if (Sched::Thread::isIdleSlot(Arch::CpuLocal::currentSlot())) {
      markIdle(true);
    }

    unlockRunQueue();
  }

  /// The run queue length and the idle mask, for a failing self-test to
  /// report.
  function queueLength(): u64 {
    return SchedulerState::queueLength;
  }

  /// Bit n set while processor n runs its idle thread.
  function idleMask(): u64 {
    return SchedulerState::idleMask;
  }

  /// Records the local APIC id of processor `cpu`, for the reschedule IPI.
  function setProcessorLapic(cpu: u64, lapicId: u64): void {
    *(&mut SchedulerState::lapicIds as *mut u64 + cpu) = lapicId;
  }

  /// Reschedule IPIs sent.
  function rescheduleIpiCount(): u64 {
    return SchedulerState::ipisSent;
  }

  /// Reschedule IPIs that switched an idle processor to a queued thread.
  function rescheduleSwitchCount(): u64 {
    return SchedulerState::ipiSwitches;
  }

  /// Appends `slot` to the run queue. The queue is a ring buffer of
  /// `MAX_THREADS` entries, so it can never overflow: a thread is queued at most
  /// once.
  function enqueue(slot: u64): void {
    let tail: u64 = (SchedulerState::queueHead + SchedulerState::queueLength) % Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS;

    *(queueBase() + tail) = slot;
    SchedulerState::queueLength += 1;
  }

  /// Removes and returns the slot at the head of the run queue.
  function dequeue(): u64 {
    let slot: u64 = *(queueBase() + SchedulerState::queueHead);

    SchedulerState::queueHead = (SchedulerState::queueHead + 1) % Sched::Thread::MAX_THREADS;
    SchedulerState::queueLength -= 1;

    return slot;
  }

  /// A pointer to the first entry of the run queue.
  function queueBase(): *mut u64 {
    return &mut SchedulerState::queue as *mut u64;
  }
}
